# Background and scheduled work

Never start a bare goroutine for work that outlives a request: gox does not stop
it on shutdown and nothing recovers its panics. Pick the mechanism, then read the
recipe named for it: `docs/recipes/<recipe>.md` in the gox module directory (the
"Look it up" section of AGENTS.md says how to find it).

## Pick one

| The work | Use | Where it runs | Recipe |
|---|---|---|---|
| Repeats on a timer; an extra or a lost run is harmless (refresh a cache, prune idempotently) | `gox.Periodic` | every replica, in process | `add-periodic-job` |
| A loop or listener with its own start and stop (queue consumer, TCP listener, watcher) | `gox.Component` | every replica, in process | `add-custom-component` |
| Must happen once per event, survive restarts and retry with backoff (send a receipt, call a webhook) | `gox/jobs`: River on Postgres | any replica that works jobs | `add-background-job` |
| A schedule that must fire once per fleet, or at a clock time (nightly at 03:00) | `jobs.Schedule` with a cron spec | one replica per run | `add-background-job` |
| Several steps over hours or days, waits for signals, compensates on failure | `gox/providers/temporal` | Temporal workers | `add-temporal-workflow` |
| API and workers deployed and scaled separately from one codebase | one wiring package, two mains | per binary | `two-binaries-one-wiring` |

Decide with these questions, in order:

1. Would running it on every replica at once be wrong (double charge, duplicate
   email, report sent N times)? Then not `gox.Periodic`: use `gox/jobs`.
2. Must it survive a deploy or a crash mid-run, or retry later? `gox/jobs`.
3. Is it one task, or a process with steps and waits? One task: `gox/jobs`.
   A process: Temporal.
4. None of the above: `gox.Periodic` for a timer, `gox.Component` for a loop.

`gox/jobs` needs Postgres
{{- if not .Postgres }}, which this service does not have yet: add it first (gox-add-feature skill)
{{- end }}. Temporal needs a Temporal server. Both are modules to `go get` (gox-add-feature skill).

## gox.Periodic

- It is an option to `gox.New`. The first run comes shortly after start, then one
  every interval; a restart resets the schedule. It is not a clock-time cron.
- The function gets a context cancelled on shutdown: pass it to every call and
  return when it is done. A returned error or a panic is logged and the next tick
  runs as usual; there is no retry or backoff.
- Make it a named function or a method on a struct that holds its dependencies,
  so a test calls it directly: `err := job.run(ctx)`. Never wait for the ticker in
  a test.
- An interval from config: `gox.Periodic` takes it before `gox.New` loads config.
  Recipe `add-periodic-job`, section "Interval from config". `jobs.Schedule` runs
  after `New`, so it reads `cfg` directly.

## gox.Component

`gox.Component(c)` in `gox.New`, or `a.Add(c)` after `New`: recipe
`add-custom-component`. The contract is in
`go doc github.com/guilhermebr/gox/pkg/lifecycle` (read it; do not import it).

## gox/jobs

- `jobs.Enable(postgres.From)` in `gox.New`; `jobs.Register` and `jobs.Schedule`
  between `gox.New` and `a.Run()`, like routes.
- Enqueue inside the transaction that makes the change: `jobs.From(a).InsertTx`
  within `postgres.Tx`, so the job exists only if the change commits.
- Never rename a job's `Kind()` once jobs of that kind exist.
- `{{ .Prefix }}_JOBS_WORK=false` makes a process insert-only: an API next to a separate
  worker. `go doc github.com/guilhermebr/gox/jobs` has the rest.

